          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"gissing-the-odd-women-186d3cf57fc4c227\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Rhoda directly presents the cessation of her love as true. In context, the avowal also helps terminate the marriage negotiation and close the relationship, giving it slight performative force, but it does not itself clearly undertake a commitment. Her emotional struggle and threatened sob do not make the controlled verbal answer an expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The narration strongly suggests that Rhoda may still love Everard and is speaking defensively or self-deceptively; this affects sincerity, not the utterance's predominantly truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Rhoda directly answers Everard's question about whether she has ceased to love him: “Yes, I no longer love you.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The statement comes after Rhoda rejects both legal marriage and a revival of their earlier unconventional ideal. Her coldly final manner presents the claim as a decisive dismissal, but the preceding agitation, the narrator's account of her internal struggle, and her immediately threatened sob undermine its likely sincerity. It may be defensive denial intended to preserve dignity and force a clean separation. Those complications do not alter the core utterance's status as an assertion of ceased love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Everard asks, “In other words, then, you have ceased to love me?” Rhoda answers, “Yes, I no longer love you.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit question-answer structure strongly supports a truth-conditional assertion that her loving state has ended.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Rhoda says she will never marry and turns toward Everard “with a cold smile,” as if finally dismissing the subject.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance participates in closing the romantic negotiation, supplying limited performative pressure while remaining primarily assertive.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier Everard interprets Rhoda as contending with herself, with honesty and dignity struggling against “the impulses of her heart”; after the exchange, “A threatened sob made her break off.”\",\"supports\":\"Rhoda's continued affect casts doubt on the truth or sincerity of her denial, but does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force in the core utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The supplied form metadata identifies the occurrence as negative, present-tense, and cessative, with the temporal modifier “no longer.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the explicit semantic form presents love as a formerly existing state that has ceased.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core force: it is predominantly a truth-conditional denial of continuing love, with minor relational-closing force. Possible insincerity, defensiveness, and narrative irony belong to contextual interpretation rather than requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification distinguishes the mode of avowal from its likely falsity or self-deceptive character.\"}"
        }
      ],
